  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Seasonable temperatures and dry through Monday,\nthen rain chances return late next week.\n\nStrong surface high remains in place across the region through the \nstart of next week. Seasonal temperatures and dry conditions are \nexpected through Monday as highs reach the 60s, and lows settle in \nthe upper 30s to 40s each day. Passing high cirrus today gives way \nto more mid-level cloudiness Sunday due to a shortwave trough that \nslides south of the region. The surface high moves offshore Monday \nand remains centered east of the Mid-Atlantic coast through the \nmiddle of next week. Return of east to southeast winds bring a \ngradual warming trend next week that has highs reach the low 70s on \nTuesday, though still remaining dry. \n\nA deep upper level trough pivots eastward from central CONUS on \nWednesday, with precipitation chances returning to the forecast \narea. At the surface, a low pressure system tracks from the \nMississippi River valley towards the Mid-Atlantic the second half of \nthe week. As the system approaches, precipitation chances increase \nand are expected to linger through the end of the week. The best \nchance for widespread rain is on Thursday afternoon into Friday, as \nthe low pressure system passes nearby. Surface high pressure builds \noverhead in the wake of the low pressure system, with diminishing \nrain chances expected. High temperatures will be in the upper 60s to \nlow 70s east of the Blue Ridge with those along and west in the \nupper 50s to 60s. Overnight low temperatures will be in the 50s each \nnight. Lows drop into the 40s east of the Blue Ridge Thursday and \nFriday night.",
